The  driver  provides support for Bluetooth SCO (Synchronous con‐
nection‐oriented) Audio devices through the driver.   The  driver
must  be  configured at run time with the program.  The following
properties are used by the driver during autoconfiguration: Local
device address.  Remote device address.  The driver  matches  the
and  services.   For  the service, the device will, on listen for
incoming connections from the remote device.  Otherwise, will at‐
tempt to initiate a connection to the remote device.  This  inte‐
ger value is not used directly, but will be stored and passed via
the ioctl as below: SCO connections require a baseband connection
between  the  two devices before they can be created.  The driver
does not create this, but can provide information  to  facilitate
an application setting up a control channel prior to use, via the
call  on  the mixer device, which returns a structure as follows:
#include <dev/bluetooth/btsco.h>

struct  btsco_info  {          bdaddr_t        laddr;          /*
controller  bdaddr  */         bdaddr_t        raddr;          /*
headset bdaddr */         uint8_t         channel;        /*  RF‐
COMM  channel */         int             vgs;            /* mixer
index speaker */         int             vgm;            /* mixer
index mic */ };

#define BTSCO_INFO      _IOR(’b’, 16, struct btsco_info) The dri‐
ver can be configured to act in Connect or Listen mode.  In  Con‐
nect  mode,  the  driver will initiate a connection to the remote
device on an call, whereas in Listen mode, will block  until  the
remote  device  initiates the connection.  The driver was written
for by under the sponsorship of Itronix, Inc.  takes no notice of
the HCI Voice Setting in the Bluetooth controller, and this  must
be  0x0060 (the default) as alternate values are currently unsup‐
ported.
